less other animators had 20  plus years of experience including  extensive work on the other three Dragon  Ball series if we were to look at  episode 5 specifically the newest  animator on that staff listing had three  or four years of experience and were  right at the bottom of the staff list  which meant that they contributed very  little to the episode itself as Toei  orders their credits by the number of  cuts drawn within the episode itself you  have animators like Nikita and Ken  Otsuka who have both 20 years of  experience in the industry and if I  continue down the staff miss the  majority of them have at least 10 years  experience but in saying that this is my  correction Super Saiyan Goku dashing  towards the camera fully corrected for  episode 81 but who needs industry  experience where you've got thread this  time-travel be skipping ahead to episode  81 deep into the final arc of the series  trevally correcting this slightly derpy  Gohan and hopefully transforming it into  a perfectly unmodeled gym as I was  saying for noir hero hayashida to  suggest that this was the work of newbie  animators feels a lot more like shifting  the blame onto the staff that worked on  the show and away from the executives  who made the decisions to rush the  series into production but why not delay  it it seems like a simple fix well as I  suggested earlier for the same reason  companies do anything money  Dragon Ball after resurrection  was right in a resurgence in popularity  with the completion of Dragon Ball Kai  bringing a newer younger audience to the  product and resurrection F being the  smash hit that it was momentum was  firmly with the Dragon Ball brand and to  ignore that wouldn't make good business  sense for the advertising networking and  merchandising potential the new series  possessed and while the decision made  good business sense and ultimately led  to super becoming the most popular and  largest moneymaker in the anime industry  during the time I was o